{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495101-mage-in-hong-kong","id":1495101,"title":"Mage in Hong Kong","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-10-17T02:05:31-07:00","id":1495101,"title":"Mage in Hong Kong","content":"Hi all<br /> <br /> Been a long time but I am going to be running Mage again. Yay! I quite fancy setting it in a city which I haven't portrayed before. One of my players suggested Hong Kong. I have a decent plot in mind that I could move anywhere, but is there any existing material on HK in Mage anywhere, and if not, has anyone with more familiarity of the city got any cool concepts to draw on?<br /> <br /> I've ordered a couple of books on the recent history of the city, but just wanted to see if the forum had any views <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-17T02:10:47-07:00","id":1495102,"title":"","content":"There\u2019s an Actual Play thread titled Eater of Names by Acrozatarim on this very forum\u2026 but it\u2019s for Werewolf. It still could be useful to get a general feel for the city.<br /> <br /> No idea about Mage, sadly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-17T06:15:03-07:00","id":1495114,"title":"","content":"Here is the thread 21C Hermit mentioned:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/925413-w-tf-actual-play-the-eater-of-names\">Eater of Names</a><br /> <br /> Both <i>Changeling: the Lost 2e</i> and <i>Hunter: the Vigil 2e</i> offer some limited material on Hong Kong, from their respective splat's viewpoint.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T00:28:09-07:00","id":1495154,"title":"","content":"I'll give that a read, as a lover of Werewolf too <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T14:54:14-07:00","id":1495193,"title":"","content":"I lived in Hong Kong back in the mid-late 2000s, I might have (out of date) flavor if you have anything specific you want to expand on. Granted, it's information mostly limited to Hong Kong island, from a foreigner's perspective, and stemming entirely from my memores of middle school, so take it with a huge grain of salt. I may also get small things mixed up with Singapore and Manila, but I think I'm catching those memory-bleeds pretty well.<br /> <br /> For example, there's four 50km+ hiking trails that cover the island cluster, two of which intersect the main city at several points. Their structure and use would make for interesting Shadow-adjascent encounters. The trails are broken up into rougly 1km legs, each of which start and end with access to roads and bus stops. They're remote, but you're unlikely to become stranded as long as you can walk the path. The Hong Kong Trail is the one that ties closest to the city - theres an annual competiton called the Green Power Hike that covers the full distance of that trail. The other three are the Maclehose Trail, the Wilson Trail, and the Lantau Trail. The only one I've never set foot on is the Lantau trail, mostly because it's on a separate island.<br /> <br /> Tied with the above, don't think of Hong Kong city as being like, say, LA. Much like Singapore, the city merges with the forest most everywhere outside of the city center. This means that Magath are likely to see a higher formation rate than other places; abominations made out of nature spirits, human emotions, and city spirits. Also worth noting is that the city parts of Hong Kong island encircle the coast, and vanish entirely the closer to the center of the island you go. Partially it's from the fact that it's been a port since it's earliest days, partially its the rough terrain. The upshot is that as a city, it's actually composed of several widely-scattered pockets of very densely packed buildings, occasionally separated by mountains or stretches of water.<br /> <br /> There's a revolving restaurant named R66 (it takes 66 minutes to do one rotation) at the top of one of the (many) skyscrapers. The Hopewell Centre, I think. Given the panoramic view of the city it provides, it might make a neat Consillium venue if rented out for big events. Given the cost and layout though, it might be more appropriate to book a few tables and ward them to hell and back.<br /> <br /> The Metro system can get you close to just about anywhere you want to go in the city, as can the buses. Taxis can get you door-to-door, but unless you're in a rush (or have tired children), you're likely to be walking or using the pulbic transit. Going <i>outside</i> the city proper gets a little more tricky, but the buses -and a taxi, if you're willing to pay out the nose- can still get you most places. Cars are for the rich, for the most part.<br /> <br /> The metro is almost entirely underground, and connects Hong Kong island to Kowloon and the rest of the New Territories. It has to pass under a stretch of shallow ocean to get there, so you could absolutely use the metro tunnels for all kinds of subway horror mixed with terrors from the deep.<br /> <br /> Cabs work on a call system - there are several companies, and most everyone learns their numbers pretty quick. If you don't think a cab will be nearby when you need it, or you want to be sure the cab you get is able to make your destination, you call the dispatch center and give them your location and desired destination. Guard shacks at apparetments can also call them for you, and will often have a policy of noting down your departure, destination, and the cab number. That record keeping can have an impact on Mage security, since the dispatch can become very familiar with your phone number and typical destinations. My memory is hazy here, but I think back in the pre-app days, you could have the dispatch center record your name and home address, to save a bit of time when calling them for a ride home. Not an outlandish system, but I've known a few places that don't use anything close to it and I don't know what you're most familiar with. I'm also not sure how Uber and other ride-share type setups have affected things.<br /> <br /> Unless the character is filthy rich or a high-ranking diplomat, they're living in an appartment. There are some really, REALLY nice appartments, but rent is always expensive. &quot;Commute from your house in the countryside where rent is cheaper&quot; also isn't really a thing. Most appartments have a guard shack that monitors vehicular traffic, and the upscale ones will have a concierge desk that will call up to your appartment when someone shows up to see you. Middle- and Upper-class appartments tend to have gates and electronic security systems, tied to either appartment-specific PINs or to custom physical electronic key cards like those you see in hotels these days.<br /> <br /> Having a live-in or daily maid is very common for the middle- and upper-class. Upper-class will also have nannies, and for the middle-class the &quot;helper&quot; sometimes fill both roles. Quite a few apparements will have space set aside for (disgustingly tiny) maid quarters. The helpers themselves are most often Thai, Malay, or Pinoy women. I don't think I ever saw a male helper. Mainland Chinese are rarely permitted in as helpers, although US diplomats (and possibly others too, but that's outside my wheelhouse) coming from assignments in Beijing, Shanghai, or similar East Asian posts will sometimes jump through the hoops to make it work. Maids/nannies can be both a boon and a bane in game-terms, since it means another Sleeper to keep track of, a potential infiltration vector, or even cover for a Sleepwalker assistant.<br /> <br /> English is understood -if not spoken- in a similar way that Spanish is in the US. Most people will have learned it at some point, but retain little once they leave school. Cantonese gets spoken more often than Mandarin the farther into the New Territories you go, and speaking Mandarin might get you a bit side-eyed if you aren't obviously a foreigner. They aren't huge fans of mainlanders, overall. This animosity is touched on in Changeling, where they talk about the Beijing Courts vs the Hong Kong Courts. And in the real-word news, which has been... understandably tumultuous the past few years.<br /> <br /> As a linguistic aside, Westerners often find Cantonese as a language sounds much angrier than the speaker actually is. This can color the background a lot - a walk through the city can be very loud and intimidating if you aren't aware of what's actually being yelled around you. For those unaware, &quot;Chinese&quot; is best used to describe the written language, since that is nearly universal as the script for the seven primary dialects. For spoken languages, the seven are different enough to often be mutually incomprehensible. &quot;Mandarin&quot; Chinese is what most people encounter outside of China, and &quot;Cantonese&quot; Chinese will be what you'll encounter the next most often when in or near Hong Kong, China, or SEA.<br /> <br /> If your characters have families, the Hong Kong International School might make for interesting plot hooks. It's about an hour outside the city center by school bus, by Tai Tam Bay, and is largely populated by the children of diplomats and foreign buisness-people. Good place for the kids of Seers and Pentacle to unknowingly rub elbows. It's also right at the edge of city, forest, and sea, so prime real-estate for a Spirit-driven game.<br /> <br /> Keeping on the vein of &quot;foreigners in Hong Kong&quot; - it's worth mentioning that diplomats (from the US at least) are often living a bit above their nominal means when on assingment. A lower-middle-class family from DC might suddenly be living at a standard that could be better described as Upper-middle-class. Their housing -and often utilities- is covered by the State Department, so they can often afford things like live-in maids. Their kids also get paid-for tuition at private schools (like HKIS), which influences spending. It also means that the State Department has a surprisingly high percentage of employees from the Mormons and other religions that encourage large families, since housing and schooling being paid for is a massive ease on the wallet when you have five or six kids close in age. Depending on how you portray religion vs the Paternoster, the poor-and-wealthy vs Mamon, and diplomacy vs the Hegemony, these factors can make for the basis of low-ranked Seers or their stooges. Or just plain Pentacle Mages, in a Cold War mentality. Bonus points on that second one if they're from the CIA.<br /> <br /> The part of the city I lived in was the Wanchai Gap - I'd characterize it as &quot;steep&quot;. Everything was built onto a slope, and you felt it when you were walking to the metro or onto the hiking trail access. Tangentially related, my pretty fit mother would stop at the top of that hiking trail access slightly winded, only to be passed by dozens of hunched old grannies going at a fair clip and not slowing a step. Old folks can be tough as nails there.<br /> <br /> Jesus, scanning the google maps view really puts scale in perspective. Everything feels so much smaller, now that I'm not in middle school.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Monkish Asexual.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1490057-cauthon-s-homebrew-hub#post1490057\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a><br /> <br /> Mostly checked out, there's an uncomfortable amount of condescension in the old guard.</div>","edited":{"userid":27258,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","user":"Cauthon","date":". 10-18-2022, 11:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","name":"Cauthon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27258_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1780"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-21T09:04:00-07:00","id":1495356,"title":"","content":"Ah that's a very useful bit of insight, thank you!<br /> <br /> I kind of want to structure the Cabals of the Pentacle as being inherent more on the traditional HK side of things with the increasing attempts by the PRC to undermine the two systems approach as benefiting the Seers (the PRC gov seems like it would be ripe for Seer uses).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-21T14:27:14-07:00","id":1495372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1495101-mage-in-hong-kong?p=1495356#post1495356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah that's a very useful bit of insight, thank you!<br /> <br /> I kind of want to structure the Cabals of the Pentacle as being inherent more on the traditional HK side of things with the increasing attempts by the PRC to undermine the two systems approach as benefiting the Seers (the PRC gov seems like it would be ripe for Seer uses).</div> </div> </div> </div>Makes sense. If your focusing on the governmental aspect, you'll mostly want to be on Hong Kong Island then. Government Hall and the foeriegn consulates are there, so a Seer trying to drive diplomatic wedges between Hong Kong and those who'd support independence would likely operate around there.<br /> <br /> Macau would be more where they'd crop up in traditional vice-and-discord roles. Macau gets a lot of gambling tourism.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Monkish Asexual.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1490057-cauthon-s-homebrew-hub#post1490057\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a><br /> <br /> Mostly checked out, there's an uncomfortable amount of condescension in the old guard.</div>","edited":{"userid":27258,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","user":"Cauthon","date":". 10-21-2022, 06:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27258-cauthon","name":"Cauthon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27258_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1780"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}